%): Boolean nibble quotient/remainder equations plus direct raw-operand normalization remove four dependent launch subtract/select stages and full-width magnitude dependencies, shifting the timing bottleneck to forwarding/ALU-to-memory with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-1\n…[2346 of 5418 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c9de66e9c4087da5]…\n retaining M-engine latency and serial divider arithmetic, so its 12.21% CoreMark gain trails the 15.39% gain from parallel divider trials.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r8s0 (regression, -11.71%): On Gowin, four-bit carry-select ADD/SUB with a three-level block prefix adds prefix, mux and routing delay to the forwarded base-ALU path that outweighs shorter local carry chains, reducing median Fmax from 73.757 to 65.115 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r8s2 (improvement, +8.43%): Separating fetch-queue occupancy/PC recovery from packed payload-bank writes keeps EX correction out of wide write enables, preserving Fmax while prediction-guided acquisition during decode holds cuts CoreMark stalls.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r9s0 (regression, +5.84%): Capturing forwarded rs1 + imm in EX/MEM.effective_addr bypasses ALU operand/operation/link selection on the dmem path, improving Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles while leaving the divider prefix as a placement-dependent timing limit.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
