{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'for p in /AGENTS.md /srv/AGENTS.md /srv/hwebench/AGENTS.md /srv/hwebench/clones/AGENTS.md /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_1-sol_xhigh-v2-rep4/AGENTS.md /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_1-sol_xhigh-v2-rep4/cores/AGENTS.md /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_1-sol_xhigh-v2-rep4/cores/bench/AGENTS.md; do if [ -f \"$p\" ]; then cat \"$p\"; fi; done; cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r1s0 (regression, +4.37%): Sharing DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in a tapered radix-4 divider reduced logic cells by 60%, but parallel trial-subtract selection and routing kept division on the forwarding-to-ALU critical path, limiting Fmax improvement to 4.37% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 (improvement, +522.03%): A shared eight-edge divider with four restoring steps per clock breaks the combinational / and % chain feeding the EX result mux, making the Fmax gain outweigh blocking DIV/REM latency in CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r2s1 (improvement, +30.03%): Separating a four-bit launch prefix with high-divisor guards from two radix-4 stages fed by registered D/3D multiples improves divider timing by removing live-operand muxing and halving serial full-width subtract/select decisions while retaining eight-edge latency.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 (regression, +16.21%): A 64-entry direction table with decoded B/J targets cuts stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.45%, but the register-file-to-divider launch path remains critical, so its throughput gain trails retiming that path.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r3s0 (regression, +6.69%): Combining forwarding and PC/immediate selection in parallel one-hot masked-OR muxes removes serial operand selection and improves Fmax with unchanged CoreMark cycles, shifting the critical path to divider launch.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r3s1 (improvement, +10.05%): Combining explicit flip-flop RF storage with validity-only ID/EX squash removes both BSRAM read-output absorption and redirect feedback through RF addresses and operand capture, improving Fmax with unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r4s0 (regression, +11.57%): Boolean nibble quotient/remainder equations plus direct raw-operand normalization remove four dependent launch subtract/select stages and full-width magnitude dependencies, shifting the timing bottleneck to forwarding/ALU-to-memory with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-1\n…[4797 of 7869 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4cd4777af5b8d7ae]…\nles at 78.453 MHz still miss qualification.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r11s1 (regression, +1.15%): Four-bank speculative fetch recovery with a two-bit token and fixed two-level PC mux shifts the critical path to divider magnitude-to-quotient logic, limiting throughput improvement to 1.15% at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r11s2 (improvement, +6.27%): Preselecting direct recovery PCs in ID/EX and bypassing the fetch address with a registered JALR target removes live forwarding/arithmetic from fetch-PC feedback, raising Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles; accepting ready landing responses and committing unready targets preserves recovery latency across instruction-bus stalls.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r12s0 (regression, -9.63%): Moving queued/live operand selection after four parallel FF register-file reads adds 1,440 logic cells and shifts the bottleneck to forwarded operands driving fetch-PC recovery, reducing median Fmax from 87.715 to 79.265 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
