{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r12s1.yaml; cat cores/bench/experiments/run_summary.json'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-002-r12s1\ntitle: Two-bank operand delivery with queued source predecode\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |-\n  The champion scores 216.47 iter/sec at 87.715 MHz with 4,052,037 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path runs from fetch-queue occupancy through the RF into ID/EX rs2: 16.717 ns and ten cells. Queue selection currently precedes binary source decode and the complete read tree. The successful r3s1 structural attempt established explicit RF flip-flops and validity-only squash; preserve both. Isolated four-candidate RF reads preserved CoreMark cycles but used 7,256 logic cells and reached only 83.089 MHz median. Moving the high-select gate after four eight-word reductions used 5,806 cells and reached 85.950 MHz. The proposed topology instead combines a two-bank read organization with queued low-address predecode to remove occupancy-dependent decoding without four full read networks.\nhypothesis: |-\n  Restructure the fetch-to-RF operand boundary as two sixteen-word RF read banks fed by source predecode carried with queued instructions. This is one structural operand-delivery architecture. It keeps one physical 31-word flip-flop RF, two combinational read ports, one write port, the existing ID/EX capture edge and every instruction's latency. The two parts are complementary: predecode removes the binary-address comparator after queue selection; late bank selection keeps that predecode to sixteen bits per source rather than thirty-two and shares a single pair of physical read networks.\n\n  In IF compute sixteen one-hot low-offset selects for each raw source field: rs1 uses imem_data[18:15] and rs2 uses imem_data[23:20]. Predecode the raw response independently of count, stall, redirect, reset and validity. Carry the resulting 32-bit sidecar through four fixed fetch-queue banks, shifting and appending under exactly the same payload movement/enqueue conditions as instruction, PC and prediction. Append must win over shifting at its destination, including sole-entry replacement and fu\n…[5378 of 8450 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d1c6178eea9cd545]…\nected instruction across all occupancies, pop/refill, full holds, redirects, reset, bus stalls and ready/unready landing responses.\n- file: test/test_reg_file.py\n  description: |-\n    Adapt fixture wiring to independently derive predecode from binary test sources, preserving every assertion and all address-pair, same/cross-bank, write isolation, x0, bypass and synchronous reset tests.\n- file: test/test_pipeline.py\n  description: |-\n    Retain all architectural and latency checks and add source-sidecar association cases across queue/fallthrough transitions, dependency replay, WB aliases, prolonged holds, reset and wrong-path flushes.\n{\n  \"iterations\": 34,\n  \"accepted\": 12,\n  \"rejected\": 22,\n  \"broken\": 0,\n  \"broken_by_class\": {},\n  \"baseline_fitness\": 12.12,\n  \"final_fitness\": 216.47,\n  \"best_fitness\": 216.47,\n  \"best_round\": 34,\n  \"delta_pct\": 1686.056105610561,\n  \"best_lut4\": 5887,\n  \"best_ff\": 2100,\n  \"best_fmax_mhz\": 87.715,\n  \"best_iterations\": 10,\n  \"best_cycles\": 4052037,\n  \"best_ipc_coremark\": 2e-06\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
