{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_69", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat formal/checks_si.cfg; rg -n 'ten|10|eight|8|bound|divide|div' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py | tail -55; rg -n 'DIV|REM|cycles' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | head -2\"", "aggregated_output": "[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 1\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Same as checks.cfg. See checks.cfg for\n# the full rationale on what ALTOPS does and does not prove. M-extension\n# arithmetic correctness is covered by test/test_alu.py and `make formal-deep`.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n# run_all.sh rebuilds this section from the actual rtl/ glob; the list\n# below is overwritten before genchecks runs. Kept as a placeholder so\n# the section is syntactically present for any reader inspecting the cfg.\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\n1384:                       REMU(7, 5, 2), multiply(8, 7, 6),\n1398:                        if seed is None and ex_pc == 8:\n1401:                        assert not int(ex.divider_wait.value)\n1417:                assert launches == consumes == [8, 16, 28]\n1421:                _check_div_retirement(rets, program[2], aa, bb, result)\n1422:                _check_div_retirement(rets, program[3], result, b, result + b)\n1424:                _check_div_retirement(rets, program[4], result, b, low)\n1425:                _check_div_retirement(rets, program[5], low, b, low // b)\n1426:                _check_div_\n…[14147 of 17219 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5c279e76f4059c99]…\n65 iterations/second. Gowin logic cells fell from 12,312 to 4,932; registers remained 460, LUT-RAM 8, BSRAM 6, and DSP 6.\\n\\nThe timing gain is modest despite the large area reduction. The option-0 worst path still crosses forwarding and the divider, with 185.670 ns arrival versus the baseline's 199.837 ns. Its path contains 249 cells and 164 reported logic levels versus the baseline's 156 cells and 150 levels, so parallel subtract and selection routing remain material costs. The measured median improves, but the divider still dominates timing and the arithmetic tests are finite rather than an exhaustive 32-bit proof.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-01T13:13:18.335004Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r1s0 (regression, +4.37%): Sharing DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in a tapered radix-4 divider reduced logic cells by 60%, but parallel trial-subtract selection and routing kept division on the forwarding-to-ALU critical path, limiting Fmax improvement to 4.37% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
