{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -B -u - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\nimport json\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/555e5898/17f8d4bf/r8-predecode')\nr=build_all(Path.cwd(),p/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',p/'timing')\n(p/'timing-results.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2))\nprint(json.dumps(summarize(r),indent=2),flush=True)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"placement_failed\": false,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 73.488,\n  \"seeds\": [\n    68.837,\n    73.488,\n    73.488\n  ],\n  \"perturbations\": [\n    [\n      0,\n      68.837,\n      15\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      73.488,\n      13\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      73.488,\n      13\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"lut4\": 5696,\n  \"ff\": 1796,\n  \"lutram\": 0,\n  \"bsram\": 4,\n  \"dsp\": 6,\n  \"logic_levels\": 15,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/u_divider/magnitude_b_q_2_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/u_divider/remainder_q_7_s0\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -9.527,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 19.821,\n    \"logic_cells\": 19,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/u_divider\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_60", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r8s0.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-001-r8s0',\n'title':'Register one-hot EX operand and result selections before execution',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 165.86 iterations/sec at 73.757 MHz with 4,446,945 marked CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path runs from ID/EX rs1 through EX and the base ALU to dmem, at 20.005 ns through thirteen cells. Parallel divider trials removed the previous bottleneck. Round 3 showed that parallel forwarding/operand selection helps, but was limited by divider launch; round 5 registered bypass ages and preselected WB data. The current EX still decodes two-bit source selections, applies another PC/immediate mux, decodes an encoded ALU operation, and finally applies a jump-link mux. Predecode this complete selection cone while the instruction is still in ID.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Register mutually exclusive one-hot selection masks alongside ID/EX, and use balanced parallel masked-OR data selection throughout EX. This is one local mux-topology optimization with unchanged pipeline edges, instruction scheduling, branch policy, M latencies and bus behavior.\n\nIn forward_unit preserve exactly the current EX-before-MEM priority, post-trap write eligibility and x0 exclusion. Replace each registered two-bit forwarding age with three bits ordered ID/EX, EX/MEM, MEM/WB; choose 001, 010 or 100. Reset to 001 and retain the masks under exactly the existing hold condition. At the same edge register four-bit ALU operand masks: A={decoded_is_auipc,next_rs1 & {3{\"'!decoded_is_auipc}}}, B={decoded_alu_src,next_rs2 & {3{!decoded_alu_src}}}. The fourth source is the captured PC for A and immediate for B. Export the combinational decoder controls through ID ports to this sideband register; never derive next-instruction masks from the previous ID/EX payload. Kill and fetch readiness still affect only instruction validity, never these capture enables.\n\nAlso register a fourteen-bit result mask on that same edge. Bits 0..10 select ADD,SUB,AND,OR,XOR,SLT,SLTU,SLL,SRL,SRA,LUI according to the existing ALU encodings; bit 11 selects the MUL group, bit 12 the DIV/REM group, bit 13 PC+4 for JAL/JALR. All bits 0..12 require !decoded_is_jump, and bit 13 equals decoded_is_jump. Unsupported ALU encodings select no arithmetic result. Reset the result mask to ADD, matching the zeroed decoded payload. Retain this mask and operand masks through dmem and blocking-M holds.\n\nConstruct architectural rs1/rs2 as three-source masked ORs. Construct alu_a/alu_b directly as four-source masked ORs rather than forwarding followed by a separate PC/immediate mux. Branch comparisons, JALR sums, stores and captured M-extension RVFI operands must still use architectural sources. Keep EX/MEM and MEM/WB source data as the existing registered values; no live memory-data forwarding is added.\n\nSelect raw ALU results and the PC+4 link value through the registered result mask with a balanced masked-OR tree, and capture that selected value directly in EX/MEM. Remove the serial is_jump result mux after the general ALU mux. Preserve every raw arithmetic expression, M request/completion interface, sticky result and ALTOPS formula. Keep alu'\"'s standalone encoded-op behavior available via a constant parameter defaulting to that behavior; EX instantiates the predecoded mode and supplies the one-hot masks and link value. Its standalone arithmetic tests must remain meaningful, and full-core tests must exercise the predecoded mode.\n\nPreserve all pipeline bundle layouts, decoder legality, trap suppression, prediction/recovery, load-use replay, older-stage draining, retained WB forwarding, exact-once memory effects, contiguous RVFI order and the nret=1 port contract. Existing internal forwarding-age assertions must be adapted bijectively from 0/1/2 to 001/010/100 while rejecting zero-hot or multi-hot values; do not delete or weaken those assertions. Extend focused checks to operand substitution and result-mask association through kills, imem bubbles, memory holds and M holds.\n\nRun existing ALU, EX, ID and pipeline suites, unchanged formal and full-trace stalled/unstalled cosim, then canonical stalled CoreMark with all CRC/UART/bounds/marker gates. Expect exactly 4,446,945 marked cycles for ten iterations. Measure all three Gowin placement options; at unchanged cycles acceptance needs median Fmax above 77.150 MHz. Additional one-hot fanout, selector registers and nearly tied divider-prefix/redirect paths are the main risks. The predicted gain remains unmeasured until the full predecode prototype completes vendor timing.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':10,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/forward_unit.sv','description':'Register exclusive forwarding, fused PC/immediate operand and fourteen-bit ALU/link-result masks with the existing reset/hold association and producer priorities.'},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Expose the existing combinational decoder alu_op, is_jump, is_auipc and alu_src controls for sideband mask capture without changing decode legality or ID/EX payload/validity behavior.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire the new registered source/result masks and current ID decoder controls between ID, forward_unit and EX; preserve top-level IO and nret=1.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Use architectural three-source and fused four-source masked-OR operand selection; instantiate predecoded ALU result selection including PC+4, and capture its output without a trailing jump mux.'},\n {'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'Add a constant-selected predecoded result mode using existing raw arithmetic and sticky M results plus link value; preserve default encoded-op standalone mode, ALTOPS expressions and both M handshakes.'},\n {'file':'test/test_ex_stage.py','description':'Preserve isolated M retention/acceptance checks with the new one-hot inputs and add exact operand/result-mask checks including PC+4, immediate/PC substitution and holds.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Bijectively adapt existing forwarding-age assertions to exclusive one-hot masks, retaining exact source priorities and rejecting invalid masks. Cover mask/payload association under kill, missing fetch, load-use replay, dmem holds and blocking M operations.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and schema-validated:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
