{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY' > /srv/hwebench/tmp/555e5898/806fbb2b/operand_boundary_probe/timing.log 2>&1\nimport json\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom tools.eval import gowin\nprobe=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/555e5898/806fbb2b/operand_boundary_probe')\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=3) as ex:\n    futures=[ex.submit(gowin.build,Path.cwd(),probe/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',p,probe/f'gowin_p{p}') for p in (0,1,2)]\n    results=[f.result() for f in futures]\nsummary=gowin.summarize(results)\n(probe/'timing.json').write_text(json.dumps({'summary':summary,'results':results},indent=2))\nprint(json.dumps(summary,indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_54", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json, yaml, jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r3s1.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Decouple execute operand capture from squash and RAM outputs'\nd['motivation']='''The champion reaches 44.029 MHz and 98.03 fitness at 4,491,485 marked\nCoreMark cycles after the divider launch/iteration split. Its worst\npath starts in register-file storage, traverses ID, EX, IF and hazard\nlogic, and returns to ID/EX operand storage. Vendor-timed isolated probes\nexpose two coupled limits at this same boundary: removing instruction\nsquash muxes alone leaves a BSRAM-output-to-divider path at 43.961 MHz;\nan explicit flip-flop register file alone leaves EX redirect feeding\nfetch masking and register reads at 44.954 MHz. Neither single-placement\nresult justifies acceptance. The promising structural hypothesis is one\nindependent operand-capture boundary, with explicit storage and validity\ncontrol that prevents both RAM absorption and squash feedback.\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Restructure the existing fetch/decode-to-execute operand boundary so\nwide instruction/operand payload is stored independently of instruction\nvalidity, and integer register reads terminate in actual ID/EX operand\nflip-flops. These are complementary requirements of the same boundary:\nthe array-style RF permits Gowin BSRAM read-output absorption, while\nredirect-to-NOP masking feeds branch resolution back into read addresses\nand wide ID/EX update logic. Preserve stage count, the single-issue\npipeline, sequential branch policy, and the current eight-edge divider.\n\nReplace the RF array with 31 individually generated 32-bit flip-flop\nwords, each having its own synchronous reset and decoded write enable;\nx0 is constant zero. Organize the read network as four groups of eight\nregister numbers. For each of two independent ports, share a five-bit\none-hot address decoder, mask words with their selects, reduce each\neight-word group with balanced OR logic, then combine the four groups.\nUse fixed-index wiring rather than dynamic array accesses, so storage\ncannot be inferred as RAM. Both ports must read arbitrary register pairs\nwithout bank conflicts. Preserve write-first bypass on both ports and\nx0 priority. Do not add registers inside reg_file or change port latency.\n\nExpose raw imem_data and PC to decode, RF addresses and load-use matching\nwithout the current redirect/flush NOP mux. Fetch validity is imemReady,\nindependent of redirect. Set hold = dmem_stall || divider_wait and\nkill = load_use || accepted_redirect. On every nonheld edge, capture the\nraw decoded payload and operands into ID/EX, regardless of kill or fetch\nreadiness; separately set valid = fetch_valid && \"'!kill. Reset initializes\npayload and clears valid. A true hold retains both. Load-use holds PC,\nlets the load advance, and creates exactly one invalid EX slot; admit\nthe still-fetched dependent on the next available edge. Redirect kills\nthe younger slot and updates PC with existing priority over imem stalls.\nAn older unaccepted memory request holds ID/EX and suppresses EX\nadvancement and redirect. Preserve divider waits, older-stage draining,\noperand capture, sticky results and consumption exactly as today.\n\nRedirect/kill must not control IF instruction-data muxes, source read\naddresses, hazard comparison operands, or wide ID/EX payload reset,\nenable or data muxes. Only instruction validity and the fetch PC see\nsquash; do not remask raw decoded fields later and recreate the path.\nKeep conservative load-use source matching, rather than adding a source\nusage optimization. Keep existing EX/MEM divider bubbles and the rest\nof the pipeline topology. Verify the vendor netlist has explicit RF\nword and ID/EX operand flip-flops, with no register-file BSRAM instances.\n\nAudit all effect and hazard enables against their stage'\"'s valid bit.\nArbitrary invalid payloads must not launch division, redirect, issue\nmemory requests, write registers, create dependencies or retire. The\nexisting MEM/WB bypass deliberately survives WB.valid clearing on a\nmemory stall: preserve that retained result/destination behavior. On a\nnormal MEM/WB capture, qualify its stored reg_write/bypass eligibility\nwith EX/MEM.valid and existing trap suppression, so an invalid writing\nopcode cannot become a false bypass. During hold_wb, retain data, tag\nand eligibility and clear only retirement valid, exactly as before.\nDo not simply gate MEM/WB bypass by retirement valid.\n\nThis spends about 992 RF storage flip-flops plus read-selection LUTs to\nremove both measured operand-delivery limits without extra instruction\ncycles. High-fanout decode/write nets, placement congestion and the\nregistered divider recurrence are the principal remaining timing risks.\nKeep nret=1, every core port, decoder default-illegal rules, alignment\ntraps, RV32M arithmetic and ALTOPS formulas, contiguous in-order RVFI\nretirement, exact-once memory effects, and the ready-high ten-clock\nbranch-to-divide successor bound. Change no eval or formal contracts.\n\nRun unchanged unit tests, formal and canonical stalled CoreMark; extend\nRF all-address/same-group/bypass/reset tests and adversarial invalid\npayload tests. Cover killed loads, stores, divides, jumps, illegal\ninstructions and writes aliasing live operands, along with load-use\nreplay and retained WB bypass during prolonged memory stalls. Check\nall canonical CRCs and validation text, compare marker-bracketed cycles\nto 4,491,485, and inspect timing for elimination of both RF BSRAM launches\nand redirect-to-read-address feedback. Accept only median vendor Gowin\nFmax times iterations/cycle more than 4.6% above 98.03. Prototype\nmeasurements do not replace the full formal and evaluator gates.\n'''\nexisting={x['file']:x for x in d['changes']}\nd['changes']=[existing['rtl/reg_file.sv'],\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Expose raw fetched instruction and PC to decode, with fetch validity\nfrom imemReady independently of squash. Remove the redirect/flush NOP\nmux and retain PC hold and accepted-redirect priority. Add no stage.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Split wide ID/EX payload capture from validity update. Capture payload\non every edge not held by downstream dmem/divider backpressure, while\nload-use/redirect only invalidate the new slot. Retain both on a true\nhold and preserve reset. Keep raw source fields independent of kill.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'''Separate true downstream hold from younger-slot kill. Load-use still\nholds fetch PC and creates a bubble but does not hold payload storage.\nUse ready-qualified raw source fields for hazard comparisons and keep\nmemory/divider/redirect advancement priorities unchanged.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Connect ready-qualified fetch validity and separate ID hold/kill\nsignals. Audit valid qualification of hazards and side effects while\npreserving divider wiring, stage boundaries, nret=1 ports and RVFI.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Qualify captured MEM/WB reg_write/bypass eligibility with EX/MEM.valid\nand trap suppression on normal captures, preventing invalid payloads\nfrom forwarding false writes. Retain prior data, tag and eligibility\non hold_wb while clearing only retirement validity, as before.\n'''},\n existing['test/test_reg_file.py'],\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain every existing check, including the ten-clock branch-to-divide\nbound. Add same-group operands, invalid-payload live-register aliases,\nwrong-path loads/stores/divides/jumps/illegal instructions, load-use\nreplay, and retained WB forwarding across prolonged memory holds under\nrandomized bus stalls. Check RVFI operands, PC chains, contiguous order\nand exact-once accepted memory effects without modifying the harness.\n'''}]\nclass BlockDumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef represent_str(dumper,value):\n    return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',value,style='|' if '\\\\n' in value else None)\nBlockDumper.add_representer(str,represent_str)\ns=yaml.dump(d,Dumper=BlockDumper,sort_keys=False,width=76)\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(s),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\np.write_text(s)\nprint('Combined operand-boundary hypothesis written and validated.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
