{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    x=json.loads(line)\n    if x.get('category')=='structural':\n        print(x['id'], x['title'], 'fitness',x.get('fitness'),'Fmax',x.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',x.get('cycles'))\n        h=x.get('hypothesis')\n        if isinstance(h,dict): print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\n        print(x.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 Move division out of the combinational EX path fitness 75.39 Fmax 33.863 cycles 4491485\nReplace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU hardware with one shared\nblocking divider integrated into EX. Use a registered unsigned restoring\nquotient/remainder datapath with four fixed restoring steps per clock:\neight iteration clocks replace the 32-step combinational division chain.\nNormalize signed operands and execute the first four-bit chunk on the\nlaunch edge, then execute the remaining seven chunks on subsequent edges.\nLatch the forwarded operands and operation, and correct quotient/remainder\nsigns from registered results without adding a correction state.\nPreserve division by zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics exactly. MUL variants\nand ordinary ALU operations retain their existing single-cycle behavior.\n\nAdd explicit launch, busy, result-valid and consume handshakes. Launch\nonly for a valid divide/remainder in EX when downstream backpressure\npermits it. Hold PC and ID/EX until the result can enter EX/MEM. Older\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB instructions must drain once; emit invalid EX/MEM\nbubbles during division rather than repeatedly holding a retiring or\nmemory-accessing instruction. A genuine dmem stall instead holds its\npending EX/MEM request. Retain a completed divider result until accepted,\nand never restart the held instruction. Latch the forwarded rs1/rs2 values\nat launch so changing bypass sources during the wait cannot corrupt the\nresult or RVFI operands. Reset cancels all divider state.\n\nKeep RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas identical to the existing contract,\nwith the same bounded divider handshake and iteration latency as real\nhardware. The first chunk must count toward the eight iteration clocks;\ndo not add separate setup or correction clocks. Consume the completed\nresult into EX/MEM on the next edge when downstream is ready. This keeps\neven a taken branch followed by division within the unchanged ten-clock\nsuccessor-retirement window under the formal wrapper's ready-high buses.\nDo not change form\n…[8853 of 11925 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:103d3fc6bccc16fb]…\nx times iterations/cycle more than 4.6% above 98.03. Prototype\nmeasurements do not replace the full formal and evaluator gates.\n\nA temporary RTL prototype of this complete operand-boundary change\npassed all 18 existing pipeline tests, all five RF unit tests, and\n320 additional seeded sequences checking killed side effects and\ninvalid load-use payloads. The unchanged CoreMark harness with both\nrandom bus stalls completed in exactly 4,491,485 marked cycles, without\nout-of-bounds accesses, with all canonical CRCs and validation text.\nVendor builds for placement options 0/1/2 returned 48.521/47.279/47.279\nMHz, giving 47.279 MHz median and implied fitness 105.26, or +7.38%\nagainst 98.03. Option 0 used 4,100 logic cells, 1,538 registers, four\nBSRAMs and six DSPs; the RF BSRAM/read-output path and IF squash chain\ndisappeared from its worst paths. Its new worst path is 25.904 ns\nthrough forwarding and divider launch. These are probe results on a\ntemporary source copy, not a completed implementation or formal pass.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
