bits, then ascending (value,mask).\nEmit selected cubes in ascending (value,mask) order as explicit bitwise\nAND terms joined by OR. Bit zero of x is P[0], bit seven is D[3]. This\nproduces 21,22,17,8,27,10,4,2 product terms for R[0:3] then Q[0:3].\nNo ROM initializer, indexed constant array, dynamic table shift or\nvariable arithmetic divider is needed. Confirm the vendor netlist\nretains four total BSRAMs, zero LUT-RAMs and 1,538 registers, matchi\n…[2349 of 5421 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f57ba31f63daa240]…\nal\nYosys SAT proof without ALTOPS proved direct top-nibble normalization,\nexact small-divisor classification and low-nibble normalization for\narbitrary raw operands, the launch quotient/remainder identities and\nboth existing full-width recurrence invariants. The unchanged CoreMark\nharness with random instruction/data stalls completed in exactly\n4,491,485 marked cycles with all canonical CRCs and validation text,\nvalid timing brackets and no out-of-bounds accesses.\n\nGowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 51.960/54.059/54.059 MHz on this\nprototype, yielding 54.059 MHz median and implied fitness 120.36, or\n+11.57 percent versus the champion. Option zero used 4,184 logic cells,\n1,538 registers, zero LUT-RAMs, four BSRAMs and six DSPs. Its worst path\nmoved out of divider launch to the forwarding/ALU-to-memory path,\nwith 24.540 ns arrival and 13 cells. These are isolated prototype\nmeasurements; the implementing agent must reproduce the change and\nrun every unchanged evaluator gate, including full pipeline formal.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
