{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n x=json.loads(line)\n if x.get('category')=='structural':\n  h=x.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(json.dumps({k:x.get(k) for k in ('id','title','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','critical_path')}, indent=2))\n  print(h.get('hypothesis','')[:2200] if isinstance(h,dict) else h[:2200])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261001-002-r1s1\",\n  \"title\": \"Move division out of the combinational EX path\",\n  \"fitness\": 75.39,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 33.863,\n  \"cycles\": 4491485,\n  \"lut4\": 2779,\n  \"ff\": 564,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/u_divider/remainder_q_2_s0\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -24.599,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 34.893,\n    \"logic_cells\": 109,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_id\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/u_divider\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\nReplace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU hardware with one shared\nblocking divider integrated into EX. Use a registered unsigned restoring\nquotient/remainder datapath with four fixed restoring steps per clock:\neight iteration clocks replace the 32-step combinational division chain.\nNormalize signed operands and execute the first four-bit chunk on the\nlaunch edge, then execute the remaining seven chunks on subsequent edges.\nLatch the forwarded operands and operation, and correct quotient/remainder\nsigns from registered results without adding a correction state.\nPreserve division by zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics exactly. MUL variants\nand ordinary ALU operations retain their existing single-cycle behavior.\n\nAdd explicit launch, busy, result-valid and consume handshakes. Launch\nonly for a valid divide/remainder in EX when downstream backpressure\npermits it. Hold PC and ID/EX until the result can enter EX/MEM. Older\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB instructions must drain once; emit invalid EX/MEM\nbubbles during division rather than repeatedly holding a retiring or\nmemory-accessing instruction. A genuine dmem stall instead holds its\npending EX/MEM request. Retain a completed divider result until accepted,\nand never restart the held instruction. Latch the forwarded rs1/rs2 values\nat launch so changing bypass sources during the wait cannot corrupt the\nresult or RVFI operands. Reset cancels all divider state.\n\nKeep RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas identical to the existing contract,\nwith the same bounded divider handshake and iteration latenc\n…[13138 of 16210 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:186dabce51fa7c44]…\ndshake registers inside alu.sv so its existing product\nstructure and DSP mapping are retained.\nAccept the result into EX/MEM on the following available edge. This\nadds two blocked cycles to multiplication relative to today's live\ncombinational multiply but removes its DSP/correction/result-selection\nchain from forwarding-to-memory timing. Do not duplicate a live\nmultiplier in the ordinary ALU result cone.\n\nFor division, retain exactly eight edges from accepted launch to\nresult_valid. Edge 1 captures unsigned magnitudes, sign metadata and\noperation; perform neither prefix quotient work nor 3D generation\non that edge. Edge 2 consumes the first eight dividend bits using\nonly registered magnitudes and registers Q, R, D and 34-bit 3D.\nImplement the first four quotient bits as explicit Boolean/narrow\narithmetic logic rather than a dynamic-index ROM or 256-entry case\ntable. Use the exact arithmetic truth functions of\nx={D[3:0],A[31:28]}: for nonzero D<16, Q=P/D and R=P%D;\nfor D=0, Q=15 and R=P. Enumerate the 256 co\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
