{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_43", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r8s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261001-002-r8s1\ntitle: \"Separate load/store address generation from the general ALU\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 165.86 iterations/sec at 73.757 MHz in 4,446,945\n  marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path is 20.005 ns\n  from an ID/EX operand through EX and the ALU to a data-memory block.\n  The recent structural divider work removed the divider recurrence as\n  that path's limit. Currently every dmem address comes from the general\n  EX/MEM alu_result, so forwarding, general ALU operand selection, operation\n  selection and link-result selection can all affect a timed memory output.\n  A dedicated effective-address channel targets this newly exposed path\n  without adding dependency, branch or memory-access cycles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split load/store address generation from architectural ALU writeback.\n  Add a dedicated 32-bit effective-address register at the existing EX/MEM\n  boundary, computed directly as forwarded rs1 plus the decoded immediate.\n  This address channel must bypass the ALU's PC/rs1 and immediate/rs2\n  operand multiplexers, its operation-result mux, M-result selection and\n  JAL/JALR link selection. Forwarded rs1 retains the existing registered\n  bypass selectors and youngest-writer priority. The address arithmetic\n  must be independent of the general ALU result cone, with no new stage\n  or extra load-use bubble. Allow the dedicated register's raw data to\n  capture whenever EX/MEM advances; request validity and memory controls\n  qualify side effects separately. Reset clears it, and a genuine dmem\n  stall holds it with the pending memory instruction.\n\n  Route dmem_addr, alignment checks, byte-position extraction and RVFI\n  aligned memory address exclusively from the dedicated effective address.\n  Preserve full 32-bit wrapping rs1+imm arithmetic and existing byte,\n  halfword and word semantics. Keep alu_result authoritative for ordinary\n  arithmetic, links, M results and EX/MEM forwarding; loads still select\n  their extracted data before MEM/WB, and th\n…[1806 of 4878 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:dd134823a227180c]…\n association through bubbles and M waits.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use effective_addr for the dmem address, memory alignment, byte-lane\n      positions, load extraction and RVFI aligned address. Preserve general\n      ALU/load writeback, trap suppression, retained WB forwarding and all\n      exact-once memory and retirement behavior.\n  - file: test/test_ex_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt bundle unpacking for the new address field while retaining\n      every existing assertion. Check independent effective-address\n      arithmetic with RF, EX/MEM and WB forwarded bases, signed immediates,\n      32-bit wraparound, reset and downstream address retention.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all tests and add directed forwarded load/store bases and\n      mixed ALU/link/M instructions between memory requests under both\n      bus stalls. Check byte/halfword/word addressing and misalignment,\n      RVFI addresses/results/order and exact-once store effects.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
