{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r8s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261001-002-r8s1\ntitle: \"Partition execute into fast address and late multiply paths\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Registered M operands improved timing, but the champion's two-edge\n  multiplier blocks EX even for independent following instructions.\n  The r6 structural change increased marked CoreMark cycles by 4.40%,\n  from 4,259,699 to 4,446,945, while separating the multiplier from live\n  bypasses. The champion reaches 73.757 MHz and 165.86 iterations/sec.\n  Its worst path runs through ordinary EX/ALU execution toward dmem.\n  Mapping multiplication onto existing EX/MEM operand and MEM/WB result\n  boundaries can preserve registered DSP inputs while removing multiply\n  results from the EX mux and multiplication from blocking-M control.\n  Separate load/store addressing is the fast half of this partition:\n  memory requests need only forwarded rs1+imm, yet their addresses\n  currently traverse the general ALU operation and link-result muxes.\nhypothesis: |\n  Make MUL, MULH, MULHU and MULHSU ordinary pipelined instructions that\n  execute their arithmetic in MEM instead of holding EX. At the normal\n  EX/MEM edge capture forwarded architectural rs1, rs2, operation and\n  instruction metadata. Compute the selected product only from those\n  registered operands during MEM and capture its final 32-bit value\n  directly into the existing MEM/WB selected writeback register.\n  These two existing edges provide operand and result capture; add no\n  pipeline stage and never send live EX bypasses through a multiplier.\n  Independent instructions and successive independent multiplies may\n  advance every cycle. Keep one in-order retirement on RVFI channel 0\n  at the normal MEM/WB boundary.\n\n  Partition the existing EX/MEM boundary into independent architectural\n  result and LSU effective-address channels. Add a 32-bit effective_addr\n  field, captured directly from forwarded rs1+imm with a dedicated adder.\n  Route dmem_addr, alignment checks, lane position, load extraction and\n  RVFI aligned memory address from this chan\n…[5573 of 8645 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bc9d1826aadf45fa]…\nserve registered forwarding selections and the unchanged nret=1\n      memory/RVFI interface.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve every exact M-extension vector and divider handshake\n      assertion. Exercise the physical MEM multiplier with independent\n      signed/unsigned arithmetic expectations and seeded random operands.\n  - file: test/test_ex_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Retain divider blocked-completion and invalid/illegal checks.\n      Adapt multiply-only handshake expectations to normal EX/MEM\n      acceptance, forwarded capture, downstream holds and reset. Update\n      bundle unpacking and assert dedicated effective-address arithmetic.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain architectural assertions and test independent consecutive\n      multiplies, one-bubble dependent replay, mixed M/load/store chains,\n      signed high halves, x0, wrong paths and both bus stalls. Check RVFI\n      operands/results/order, retained bypasses and exact-once stores.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
