{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_55", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r10s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261001-003-r10s2\ntitle: \\\"Cached local-history direction prediction with parallel bank training\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion uses static backward-taken/forward-not-taken prediction and a\n  four-entry predicted-path queue. The prior 32-entry agreement predictor saved\n  about 2 percent in marked cycles but lost FPGA timing. A serial history-table\n  and pattern-table lookup risks repeating that failure. Precomputing each\n  bank's next agreement bit moves history-dependent work onto the registered\n  training path and gives fetch only a shallow one-bit lookup.\n\n  A canonical ten-iteration reference trace counted 111,175 static conditional\n  direction misses. An immediate-update model of 64 independent banks, each\n  with two local-history bits and four two-bit agreement counters, counted\n  40,412 misses, versus 47,235 for the prior 32-entry agreement table. This is\n  an architectural trace estimate, not a stalled RTL or FPGA measurement;\n  queue lookahead and delayed training can reduce the gain. The expected cycle\n  benefit alone is below the acceptance margin, so the cached lookup must also\n  preserve or improve placement timing. At unchanged cycles qualification\n  requires more than 81.065 MHz; a 2.2 percent cycle reduction lowers that\n  requirement to approximately 79.28 MHz.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only conditional direction selection with one cached local-history\n  predictor. Use 64 banks indexed by PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Each bank holds a\n  two-bit resolved direction history H, four two-bit saturating agreement\n  counters C[0..3], and one cached agreement bit A. Reset H to zero, all C to\n  strong agreement (3), and A to one. For a legal aligned conditional branch\n  predict instr[31] XOR \"'!A[fetch_index]. Keep the existing aligned B/J target\n  addition, JAL always taken, JALR sequential, and legality/alignment guards.\n  No history or pattern lookup may be placed between fetch PC and prediction:\n  fetch reads only the 64 cached bits through decoded selects and a balanced\n  OR tree. This is one local-history mechanism, without a BTB or target cache.\n\n  Train from registered EX/MEM metadata exactly once when ex_mem_w.valid &&\n  !reset && !hold_mem_wb && ex_mem_w.ctrl.is_branch &&\n  !ex_mem_w.ctrl.is_illegal && ex_mem_w.pc[1:0]==0 && !ex_mem_w.instr[8].\n  For the update bank compute agreement G=(branch_taken==instr[31]), update\n  only C[H] with saturation toward G, and shift the actual direction into\n  Hnext={H[0],branch_taken}. On that same edge cache Cnext[Hnext][1]. If Hnext\n  equals old H, bypass the newly updated counter MSB; otherwise select the\n  existing counter at Hnext. This forwarding is required for repeated all-zero\n  and all-one histories. Preserve other banks and patterns. Adjacent accepted\n  updates to one bank must observe the preceding edge'\"'s new state.\n\n  Implement every bank and pattern with fixed flip-flop words and independent\n  decoded enables: 128 history bits, 512 counter bits and 64 cached bits, for\n  704 predictor state bits. Use constant-index masked reads inside each bank\n  so neither history nor pattern state infers RAM. For old counter bits h,l,\n  the exact saturating next bits are hnext=(h&l)|(G&(h|l)) and\n  lnext=(h&~l)|(G&(h|~l)). Every bank's next-context selection is local and\n  parallel; avoid a global multiplexed history read followed by a global\n  counter read. All training inputs are registered branch metadata, not live\n  forwarded EX operands, redirect or instruction-bus acceptance.\n\n  Resolved history is deliberately nonspeculative. No history rollback or\n  extra queue metadata is needed. EX must compare the actual direction with\n  the exact predicted_taken already captured with each accepted fetch, never\n  recompute its prediction from current state. Delayed updates and aliases\n  affect accuracy only. Preserve occupancy-only queue recovery, validity-only\n  kills, accepted correction priority, bus handshakes, forwarding, load-use\n  and multiply replay, M arithmetic/ALTOPS, decoder legality, alignment traps,\n  nret=1 ports, resolved architectural RVFI next PC and contiguous retirement.\n\n  Verify all counter states against independent saturating integer arithmetic,\n  every history transition, same-context bypass, unchanged future-context\n  selection, same-bank aliases, consecutive registered updates, no update on\n  holds, reset, and cold static behavior. Retain exhaustive B/J target and\n  reserved-encoding checks. Pipeline cases must combine changing predictor\n  state with queued instructions, loop exits, forward branches, every existing\n  hold reason and wrong-path stores, checking full RVFI PCs, operands, order\n  and exact-once memory effects. Run unchanged lint, formal and cosim gates,\n  canonical bracketed CoreMark CRC/UART/OOB validation and all three Gowin\n  placement options. Qualification requires fitness above 200.058 iter/sec;\n  increased FF placement density and cached-update routing are the main risks.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n    description: \\\"Add 64 fixed local-history banks with four agreement counters and a cached next agreement bit per bank; use local parallel training, exact same-context update forwarding, Boolean saturating transitions and a shallow cached-bit fetch lookup. Preserve B/J targets and legal/aligned control guards.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Wire predictor clock/reset and one accepted training event from existing registered EX/MEM PC, instruction, branch_taken and post-trap validity. Preserve every pipeline bundle, top-level port and retirement contract.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py\n    description: \\\"Retain exhaustive direct target, legality and alignment checks; add an independent history/counter/cache model for every state transition, same-context bypass, aliases, consecutive updates, holds and reset.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \\\"Preserve architectural assertions and check prediction state changes behind queued fetches, all hold reasons, loop exits, forward branches, RVFI continuity and absence of wrong-path stores.\\\"\n''')\nprint(p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
