{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_18", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git status --short --untracked-files=all; git diff --exit-code -- cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/test; git check-ignore cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r1s2.yaml; sed -n '1,150p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r1s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r1s2.yaml\nid: hyp-20261001-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Small tagged next-PC predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  This core has no branch prediction: every taken conditional branch and every\n  JAL/JALR redirects from EX and discards the current fetch, even after a loop\n  or call site has executed many times. Its IF/ID bundle is combinational, so\n  the actual penalty is one younger instruction slot per redirect, rather\n  than the two-cycle penalty suggested by the five-stage comments. CoreMark's\n  repeated loops and call sites can recover these slots with a small learned\n  next-PC predictor. The measured critical path is through forwarding and\n  the combinational M-extension ALU; a shallow predictor lookup is expected\n  to improve iterations/cycle without extending that arithmetic path.\n\n  A reference-ISS profile of the canonical 2K, -O3, ten-iteration CoreMark\n  run, bounded by its start/stop MMIO stores, counted 2,922,016 instructions,\n  529,588 conditional branches, 49,367 JALs, and 13,735 JALRs. The baseline\n  incurs 357,527 taken-control redirects. An architectural trace model of\n  this 32-entry predictor produced 73,619 next-PC misses, saving 283,908\n  instruction slots; against the recorded 4,491,485 stalled baseline cycles\n  that corresponds to approximately 6.75% throughput gain at constant Fmax.\n  A 64-entry table improved that estimate only to 7.03%, making 32 entries\n  the stronger area/timing tradeoff. This is a trace estimate, with immediate\n  predictor updates and without replaying pipeline stalls; it is not an RTL\n  simulation or a measured FPGA improvement. The reference run matched all\n  five canonical CRCs and the required UART validation text.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one 32-entry, direct-mapped, fully tagged branch-target buffer with\n  per-entry two-bit saturating conditional-branch direction counters. Index\n  with fetch PC[6:2], compare PC[31:7], and store aligned target[31:2], valid,\n  an unconditional-control-flow b\n…[3334 of 6406 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5694cf3f0bf8e8b0]…\nolds; bubbles invalidate the entire instruction and metadata together.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compare the resolved architectural next PC against predicted_next_pc for every valid advancing instruction; emit correction only on mismatch and provide one accepted resolution event for predictor training.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate and wire predictor lookup, training, metadata, and correction without changing the nret=1 top-level port set or RVFI retirement contract.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Interpret redirect as an accepted prediction correction; retain existing load-use and bus-stall handling while allowing correctly predicted control transfers to flow without a flush.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add branch_predictor.sv to the test runner's explicit source list and add directed predictor recovery and backpressure cases that check architectural retirement and wrong-path side effects; retain the existing pipeline checks.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
