{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n    e=json.loads(line)\n    if e.get('category') \"'!= '\"'predictor': continue\n    print('\\\\nID:',e['id'])\n    for key in ['title','outcome','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles','critical_path']:\n        print(key+':',e.get(key))\n    h=e.get('hypothesis',{})\n    if isinstance(h,dict):\n        print('motivation:',h.get('motivation',''))\n        print('hypothesis:',h.get('hypothesis',''))\n    print('implementation_notes:',e.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID: hyp-20261001-003-r1s2\ntitle: Small tagged next-PC predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis\noutcome: regression\nfitness: 13.26\ndelta_pct: 9.41\nfmax_mhz: 5.579\ncycles: 4208674\ncritical_path: {'from': 'cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs2_val_0_s100', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -185.654, 'arrival_ns': 195.948, 'logic_cells': 147, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nmotivation: This core has no branch prediction: every taken conditional branch and every\nJAL/JALR redirects from EX and discards the current fetch, even after a loop\nor call site has executed many times. Its IF/ID bundle is combinational, so\nthe actual penalty is one younger instruction slot per redirect, rather\nthan the two-cycle penalty suggested by the five-stage comments. CoreMark's\nrepeated loops and call sites can recover these slots with a small learned\nnext-PC predictor. The measured critical path is through forwarding and\nthe combinational M-extension ALU; a shallow predictor lookup is expected\nto improve iterations/cycle without extending that arithmetic path.\n\nA reference-ISS profile of the canonical 2K, -O3, ten-iteration CoreMark\nrun, bounded by its start/stop MMIO stores, counted 2,922,016 instructions,\n529,588 conditional branches, 49,367 JALs, and 13,735 JALRs. The baseline\nincurs 357,527 taken-control redirects. An architectural trace model of\nthis 32-entry predictor produced 73,619 next-PC misses, saving 283,908\ninstruction slots; against the recorded 4,491,485 stalled baseline cycles\nthat corresponds to approximately 6.75% throughput gain at constant Fmax.\nA 64-entry table improved that estimate only to 7.03%, making 32 entries\nthe stronger area/timing tradeoff. This is a trace estimate, with immediate\npredictor updates and without replaying pipeline stalls; it is not an RTL\nsimulation or a measured FPGA improvement. The reference run matched all\nfive canonical CRCs and the required UART validation text.\n\nhypothesis: Add one 32-entry, direct-mapped, fully tagged branch-target buffer with\nper-entry two\n…[11667 of 14739 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0aa602137f981385]…\nlaunch/consume, operand capture,\neight-edge latency, drain, and sticky-result behavior, and preserve\nretained MEM/WB forwarding during dmem stalls. Keep the nret=1 port\ncontract, decoder default-illegal behavior, alignment traps, exact-once\nmemory effects, and in-order RVFI retirement. Extend existing pipeline\ntests for warmup/saturation, loop exits, forward branches, consecutive\npredictions, JAL, JALR, aliases, reserved encodings, misaligned targets,\nand reset. Exercise prediction and correction with load-use/divider\nwaits and randomized instruction/data backpressure; check RVFI PC\nchains, operands, +1 order, and absence of wrong-path stores. Run the\nunchanged correctness gates and canonical stalled CoreMark validation,\nthen evaluate median Gowin Fmax times measured iterations/cycle.\nWith the previous measured cycle reduction, exceeding the 4.6%\nacceptance threshold requires retaining roughly 43.1 MHz or better;\npreserve timing by using one-bit recovery and a compact direction table.\n\nimplementation_notes: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
