{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n e=json.loads(line)\n if e.get('category') == 'predictor':\n  h=e.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('\\\\nID', e['id'], 'fitness',e.get('fitness'),'fmax',e.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',e.get('cycles'),'outcome',e.get('outcome'))\n  print('HYPOTHESIS\\\\n'+(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else str(h)))\n  print('NOTES\\\\n'+e.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 fitness 13.26 fmax 5.579 cycles 4208674 outcome regression\nHYPOTHESIS\nAdd one 32-entry, direct-mapped, fully tagged branch-target buffer with\nper-entry two-bit saturating conditional-branch direction counters. Index\nwith fetch PC[6:2], compare PC[31:7], and store aligned target[31:2], valid,\nan unconditional-control-flow bit, and the counter. A miss predicts PC+4;\na tagged hit predicts the stored target for JAL/JALR or a conditional\ncounter with its high bit set. Allocate legal, aligned control transfers\nwhen they advance through EX; initialize new conditional entries weakly\ntoward the observed outcome and update matching entries with saturation.\nRefresh the resolved target for each accepted JALR. Use a modest table and\nasynchronous lookup, reset only valid bits, and leave tag/target/counter\npayload unreset to allow compact FPGA storage inference.\n\nCarry the exact predicted next PC alongside each accepted instruction\nthrough IF and ID/EX. Advance the fetch PC to this prediction only when\nthat instruction is actually admitted to ID/EX. In EX compute the same\narchitectural next PC as today, including sequential PC+4 on the existing\nmisalignment/trap path, and redirect only when it differs from the carried\nprediction. Compare for every valid instruction, including nonbranches,\nso a stale target entry cannot silently redirect a changed instruction.\nCorrect predictions must neither flush nor bubble the following fetch;\nwrong direction and wrong target both recover to the resolved next PC.\n\nQualify resolution, correction, and predictor training with valid EX and\n!stall_ex_mem, so a held instruction cannot redirect or train repeatedly.\nKeep correction higher priority than an instruction-bus stall or a younger\nload-use stall. During a data-bus stall hold fetch, ID/EX, and prediction\nmetadata; delay correction until EX can advance. Preserve MEM/WB operand\nretention for forwarding. Suppress allocation for illegal or misaligned\ntransfers and invalidate a matching stale entry when EX resolves a\nnon-\n…[11480 of 14552 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b0cc47aca94303a5]…\nmisaligned B/J targets, reset, and PC\nwraparound. Combine correction with instruction/data backpressure,\nload-use stalls, forwarding, and divider waits. Check RVFI PC continuity,\noperands/results, contiguous order, and absence of wrong-path stores.\nRun unchanged formal and cosim gates and marker-bracketed canonical\nCoreMark with all five CRCs and required validation text.\n\nThe measured cycle gain is 5.44%, slightly less than the dynamic table,\nbut the static prototype adds only 106 logic cells and two registers\nin the timed wrapper (4,261 logic / 1,540 registers versus the recorded\n4,155 / 1,538). At 4,259,699 cycles, clearing the 4.6% acceptance margin\nrequires approximately 48.07 MHz median Fmax. Verify all three Gowin\nplacement options; the prototype's 49.231 MHz median clears this margin,\nwhile its lower option-0 result makes timing confirmation important.\nThe main risks are fetch-target adder routing and changed placement of\nthe unchanged forwarding-to-divider path, not predictor state accuracy.\n\nNOTES\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"The dynamic predictors saved only about 1.4% more cycles than the current static predictor, and their timing cost erased that gain. I’m looking at a smaller mechanism that retains static prediction by default and learns recurring exceptions."}}
