{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\nID',d['id'],d['title'])\n  print({k:d.get(k) for k in ['outcome','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff']})\n  if d['id'].endswith(('r3s2','r4s2','r5s2')):\n   h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n   if isinstance(h,dict): print(h.get('motivation','')); print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\n   print('NOTES:',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 Small tagged next-PC predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 13.26, 'delta_pct': 9.41, 'fmax_mhz': 5.579, 'cycles': 4208674, 'lut4': 12907, 'ff': 589}\n\nID hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 Direction-only branch prediction with decoded direct targets\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 87.61, 'delta_pct': 16.21, 'fmax_mhz': 36.81, 'cycles': 4201740, 'lut4': 3277, 'ff': 789}\n\nID hyp-20261001-003-r3s2 Bimodal direct prediction with one-bit EX recovery\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 103.09, 'delta_pct': 5.16, 'fmax_mhz': 43.316, 'cycles': 4201740, 'lut4': 3252, 'ff': 787}\nThe current champion reaches 98.03 iterations/second at 44.029 MHz,\nbut still fetches sequentially and discards one younger instruction\nslot for each taken branch or jump. The previous 64-entry direction\npredictor reduced marker-bracketed stalled CoreMark cycles from\n4,491,485 to 4,201,740, a 6.90% throughput opportunity, but was evaluated\non the older divider and reached only 36.810 MHz. Start from the current\nchampion, including its retimed radix-4 divider. Its reported worst path\nnow crosses EX branch recovery, IF instruction masking, the hazard unit,\nand the ID register. Predictor accuracy must therefore be obtained with\nshallow recovery logic, rather than a wide next-PC comparison or a\nredirect-dependent instruction mux feeding hazard/decode logic.\n\nAdd one 64-entry PC-indexed bimodal direction predictor for legal\nconditional branches, with two-bit saturating counters and a weakly\nnot-taken cold state. Index by PC[7:2], reset only an initialized bitmap,\nand leave counter payload unreset. On a cold update saturate from the\nvirtual cold state. Train exactly once when a legal, nonfaulting branch\nadvances through EX. Use the currently fetched instruction to recognize\nlegal BRANCH funct3 values {0,1,4,5,6,7} and JAL; predict JAL taken and\nJALR sequential. Compute direct targets from the instruction's B/J\nimmediate and fetch PC, preferably as an aligned 30-bit word-address\naddition. Predict t\n…[11696 of 14768 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f89b7d233a6a9611]…\nluding sequential recovery on a trap.\n\nThis is one direction-prediction change. Preserve nret=1, all ports,\nraw instruction/source-address paths, validity-only squash, forwarding,\nretained WB bypass data, divider timing and ALTOPS behavior, decoder\nlegality, misalignment traps, and exact-once memory/retirement effects.\nValidate cache misses, both static directions, confidence saturation,\nconflicting full tags, replacement, update delay, back-to-back branches,\nreset with a pending update, and correction overlapping instruction,\ndata, load-use, and divider waits. Retain all existing tests and run\nunchanged formal/cosim plus canonical marker-bracketed CoreMark and\nmedian Gowin timing. The estimated overall gain depends on containing\nlookup/update timing costs; no FPGA gain has yet been measured.\n\nNOTES: \n\nID hyp-20261001-003-r6s2 Prediction-guided fetch lookahead during pipeline stalls\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 141.48, 'delta_pct': 7.0, 'fmax_mhz': 57.396, 'cycles': 4056799, 'lut4': 4364, 'ff': 1633}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The earlier direction tables saved only about 1% beyond static prediction and reduced Fmax. The two-entry fetch lookahead saved about 5% of cycles. I’ll focus on a deeper, registered fetch queue that follows the existing predictor and buffers instructions during pipeline holds."}}
