{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'], d['title'])\n  print({k:d.get(k) for k in ('outcome','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','error')})\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  if isinstance(h,dict):\n   print(h.get('motivation',''))\n   if d['id'].endswith(('r2s2','r5s2')): print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\n  print('NOTES:',d.get('implementation_notes','')[:8000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'], d['title'])\n  print({k:d.get(k) for k in ('outcome','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','error')})\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  if isinstance(h,dict):\n   print(h.get('motivation',''))\n   if d['id'].endswith(('r2s2','r5s2')): print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\n  print('NOTES:',d.get('implementation_notes','')[:8000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 Small tagged next-PC predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 13.26, 'delta_pct': 9.41, 'fmax_mhz': 5.579, 'cycles': 4208674, 'lut4': 12907, 'ff': 589, 'error': None}\nThis core has no branch prediction: every taken conditional branch and every\nJAL/JALR redirects from EX and discards the current fetch, even after a loop\nor call site has executed many times. Its IF/ID bundle is combinational, so\nthe actual penalty is one younger instruction slot per redirect, rather\nthan the two-cycle penalty suggested by the five-stage comments. CoreMark's\nrepeated loops and call sites can recover these slots with a small learned\nnext-PC predictor. The measured critical path is through forwarding and\nthe combinational M-extension ALU; a shallow predictor lookup is expected\nto improve iterations/cycle without extending that arithmetic path.\n\nA reference-ISS profile of the canonical 2K, -O3, ten-iteration CoreMark\nrun, bounded by its start/stop MMIO stores, counted 2,922,016 instructions,\n529,588 conditional branches, 49,367 JALs, and 13,735 JALRs. The baseline\nincurs 357,527 taken-control redirects. An architectural trace model of\nthis 32-entry predictor produced 73,619 next-PC misses, saving 283,908\ninstruction slots; against the recorded 4,491,485 stalled baseline cycles\nthat corresponds to approximately 6.75% throughput gain at constant Fmax.\nA 64-entry table improved that estimate only to 7.03%, making 32 entries\nthe stronger area/timing tradeoff. This is a trace estimate, with immediate\npredictor updates and without replaying pipeline stalls; it is not an RTL\nsimulation or a measured FPGA improvement. The reference run matched all\nfive canonical CRCs and the required UART validation text.\n\nNOTES: \nhyp-20261001-003-r2s2 Direction-only branch prediction with decoded direct targets\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 87.61, 'delta_pct': 16.21, 'fmax_mhz': 36.81, 'cycles': 4201740, 'lut4': 3277, 'ff': 789, 'error': None}\nThe champion already removes combinational division\n…[13394 of 16466 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:852563e9f9e1961d]…\n\nthe 143.74 champion and above the 4.6 percent acceptance margin. Area was\n4,814 logic cells, 1,804 registers, zero LUT RAMs, four BSRAMs and six DSPs.\nThese are isolated prototype measurements; implementation and the unchanged\nevaluator must confirm them. The single mechanism is predictor-directed\nlookahead, retaining the existing branch direction and target calculation.\n\nThe packed-vector prototype passed all 53 original single-retire ALTOPS\nformal checks using unchanged wrapper/configuration/framework copies in\nisolated temporary staging, including liveness and PC continuity. A\n4,000-cycle independent deque-based cocotb model passed every occupancy,\nready/hold combination, simultaneous pop/refill, reset and correction.\nSelftest and all three random programs passed full RVFI comparison with\nand without instruction/data stalls. Canonical CoreMark passed all CRC,\nUART, completion, bounded-access and timing-marker checks. Formal-deep\nwas not run; this proposal leaves M-extension arithmetic unchanged.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"The prior results favor predictor-directed fetch lookahead: four queued instructions improved throughput by about 8.2% at equal Fmax. That candidate used the older divider and lost to the divider improvement now in the champion. I’ll propose adding that queue to the current champion while preserving its arithmetic; direction-table predictors showed much smaller cycle savings."}}
