{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r9s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-003-r9s2',\n 'title':'Small static-agreement predictor with isolated counter updates',\n 'category':'predictor',\n 'motivation':'''The champion has static backward-taken/forward-not-taken prediction and a\nfour-entry prediction-guided fetch queue. In a fresh canonical CoreMark\nreference execution its 529,588 conditional branches included 111,175 static\ndirection misses, including 27,650 not-taken instances at one backward branch\nwith predominantly zero-trip runs. Counting loop trips does not address the\nmain direction problem. Previous 64-entry bimodal tables and eight-entry tagged\nexception caches reduced cycles but lost FPGA timing. Store agreement with\nstatic direction, rather than absolute taken direction or tagged exceptions:\naliases between oppositely biased branches can reinforce the same agreement\nstate. Use sixteen tiny independent flip-flop counters, eliminating tags,\nvalid bitmaps, target storage, speculative history and read-modify-write RAM.\nAn immediate-update architectural trace model of this sixteen-entry agree\npredictor counted 55,124 direction misses versus 111,175 for static prediction.\nThis is an accuracy estimate, not a cycle-accurate or FPGA fitness measurement.\n''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Extend the existing direct-control predictor with one sixteen-entry static-\nagreement direction table indexed by PC[5:2]. Each two-bit saturating counter\nrepresents agreement with BTFNT: reset to 3, increment when actual direction\nmatches instruction bit 31, and decrement on disagreement. For a legal aligned\nconditional branch predict instr[31] XOR \"'!counter[1]. Keep aligned JAL always\ntaken and JALR sequential. Preserve the existing 30-bit B/J target addition and\nalignment/legality guards. Aliases affect accuracy only because direct targets\nalways come from the fetched instruction rather than cached tags or targets.\n\nImplement each counter as its own fixed two-bit flip-flop word with a constant-\nindex decoded update enable. Reset the 32 counter bits to strong agreement;\nthere is no initialized bitmap or payload RAM. Lookup needs only the counter\nMSBs: form sixteen fixed selected-disagreement terms and combine them with a\nbalanced OR tree. Do not synthesize a variable-index memory or BSRAM. Keep the\nread shallow and avoid changing the existing fetch/queue/ID payload layout.\n\nExport an accepted conditional-branch resolution event from EX with its own\nPC and actual-direction-agrees-with-static bit. Qualify it with in.valid,\n!reset, !stall_ex_mem, !divider_wait, legal branch encoding, aligned PC and an\naligned B target. Capture event validity, four-bit PC index and agreement in\npredictor-side registers; apply it to its selected word on the following edge.\nAll counter feedback consumes these registers, keeping live forwarded branch\ncomparators out of counter read/update arithmetic. Each held branch trains\nexactly once when it advances; pending accepted events drain independently of\nlater bus stalls and are canceled on reset. Adjacent accepted updates to the\nsame word must both apply in order. Train all accepted conditional branches,\nincluding correctly predicted branches, and never use a wrong-path fetch as\ntraining. Delay affects performance only.\n\nKeep the exact prediction captured with each accepted fetch in the existing\nqueue and ID/EX. EX compares this captured bit with effective resolved direct\ndirection; never recompute it from a subsequently trained counter. Preserve\none-bit correction, correction priority, queue occupancy-only recovery, raw\ninstruction paths, held forwarding results, two-edge MUL, eight-edge DIV and\nALTOPS, default-illegal decode, alignment traps, resolved RVFI next PC, exact-\nonce effects and contiguous retirement. Retain nret=1 and every top-level port.\n\nRetain exhaustive B/J target, reserved-encoding and alignment tests, adding an\nindependent agreement-counter model for cold bias, both static directions,\nsaturation, aliased branches with opposite biases, delayed/back-to-back updates\nand reset with pending work. Pipeline tests must combine queued predictions\nwith changed counters, loop exits, forward branches, and all load/M/bus holds;\ncheck architectural PCs, RVFI data/order and suppression of wrong-path stores.\nRun unchanged lint, formal/cosim, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/OOB/marker checks\nand all three Gowin options. The expected gain depends on avoiding the timing\nloss of earlier larger tables; the acceptance threshold is 188.113 iter/sec.\n'\"''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/branch_predictor.sv','description':'Add sixteen fixed two-bit static-agreement counters, balanced one-bit lookup, strong-static reset and predictor-side registered update events. Preserve legal/aligned B/J prediction and exact targets; infer no RAM or target storage.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Export one accepted legal aligned conditional-branch event, its own PC and actual agreement with static direction. Gate once on true EX advancement; preserve existing correction, traps, forwarding and arithmetic.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Connect clock/reset and EX training metadata to the predictor without changing external ports, retirement width, fetch queue, hazard paths or other datapaths.'},\n {'file':'test/test_branch_predictor.py','description':'Retain exhaustive direct target, legality and alignment assertions; add an independent agreement-state model covering bias, saturation, aliases, delayed updates, adjacent events and reset.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Preserve architectural assertions and cover predictions retained in the queue while counters change, corrections during each hold reason, exact-once effects and complete RVFI PC/order/operand checks.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=95,default_flow_style=False))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Revised, schema-valid hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
