{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['category']=='micro_opt' and d['id'].endswith('r2s0'):print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Replace each of the existing four 33-bit restoring trial subtractors\nwith an explicitly segmented carry-select subtractor. Keep the restoring\nalgorithm, four quotient bits per edge, first chunk on launch, eight\niteration edges, and sticky result-valid/consume protocol unchanged.\nThis is a local combinational arithmetic optimization; do not add stages,\nalter pipeline backpressure, or change the divider's completion latency.\n\nPartition the low 32 bits into four 8-bit blocks. For each block j,\nindependently compute the 9-bit unsigned differences Xj-Dj and Xj-Dj-1,\nrepresenting incoming borrow zero and one. All operands must be explicitly\nzero-extended before subtraction. Define block borrow-generate Gj as\nbit 8 of Xj-Dj and borrow-propagate Pj as Xj==Dj. With B0=0, compute\nevery incoming block borrow directly with a balanced prefix network:\nB1=G0; B2=G1|(P1&G0); B3=G2|(P2&G1)|(P2&P1&G0);\nB4=G3|(P3&G2)|(P3&P2&G1)|(P3&P2&P1&G0).\nSelect each block's low eight difference bits using Bj. The divisor's\nbit 32 is zero, so difference[32]=X[32]^B4 exactly reproduces the original\n33-bit modular subtraction, including its sign/underflow decision.\nRetain the existing choice between shifted remainder and difference\nusing difference[32], and the existing quotient-bit construction.\nAvoid a serial four-block borrow recurrence or a fresh full-width\nsubtract in parallel with the segmented network; either would defeat\nthe intended shortening of the timing path.\n\nThe full-width carry chain becomes parallel short native carry chains,\na small borrow-prefix network, and block muxes. Apply this same helper\nat all four restoring steps so both launch and busy feedback paths\nbenefit. Keep signed magnitude normalization and final sign correction\nas they are. Preserve divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1, unsigned high-bit\noperands, and signed remainder semantics. Preserve the ALTOPS formulas\nand their existing eight-edge handshake exactly. Keep nret=1, all traps,\nin-order retirement, memory side effects, and bus-ready handling intact.\n\nValidate subtraction against an independent 33-bit arithmetic reference,\nespecially borrow propagation through equal blocks and across bit 31.\nRun the existing ALU and pipeline tests, formal ALTOPS gate, and cosim;\nextend actual DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU vectors around byte boundaries and use\nseeded random signed and unsigned operands. ALTOPS alone cannot validate\nthe real arithmetic. Measure median Gowin Fmax times canonical stalled,\nmarker-bracketed CoreMark iterations/cycle. Marked cycles should remain\nidentical to the champion's 4,491,485 for ten iterations. The expected\ngain is from Fmax, with some additional LUTs and routing as the main risk;\nacceptance still requires more than 4.6% measured fitness improvement.\n\nA standalone bit-accurate model of the specified block arithmetic passed\n101,682 comparisons against independent 33-bit subtraction and 43,364\nRV32M quotient/remainder comparisons against independent integer\narithmetic. These include directed block boundaries, both values of the\nshifted remainder's top bit, random operands, zero divisors, and signed\noverflow. This verifies the proposed equations, not implementing RTL\nor FPGA timing; the predicted fitness gain remains unmeasured.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
