{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_65", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r12s1.yaml');d=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Two-bank operand delivery with queued source predecode'\nd['motivation']='''The champion scores 216.47 iter/sec at 87.715 MHz with 4,052,037 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path runs from fetch-queue occupancy through the RF into ID/EX rs2: 16.717 ns and ten cells. Queue selection currently precedes binary source decode and the complete read tree. The successful r3s1 structural attempt established explicit RF flip-flops and validity-only squash; preserve both. Isolated four-candidate RF reads preserved CoreMark cycles but used 7,256 logic cells and reached only 83.089 MHz median. Moving the high-select gate after four eight-word reductions used 5,806 cells and reached 85.950 MHz. The proposed topology instead combines a two-bank read organization with queued low-address predecode to remove occupancy-dependent decoding without four full read networks.'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Restructure the fetch-to-RF operand boundary as two sixteen-word RF read banks fed by source predecode carried with queued instructions. This is one structural operand-delivery architecture. It keeps one physical 31-word flip-flop RF, two combinational read ports, one write port, the existing ID/EX capture edge and every instruction's latency. The two parts are complementary: predecode removes the binary-address comparator after queue selection; late bank selection keeps that predecode to sixteen bits per source rather than thirty-two and shares a single pair of physical read networks.\n\nIn IF compute sixteen one-hot low-offset selects for each raw source field: rs1 uses imem_data[18:15] and rs2 uses imem_data[23:20]. Predecode the raw response independently of count, stall, redirect, reset and validity. Carry the resulting 32-bit sidecar through four fixed fetch-queue banks, shifting and appending under exactly the same payload movement/enqueue conditions as instruction, PC and prediction. Append must win over shifting at its destination, including sole-entry replacement and full pop/refill. Like the existing speculative payload, sidecar storage has no reset/recovery gates: occupancy alone determines reachability. Do not alter the existing packed if_id_t layout or its validity semantics.\n\nPresent head-sidecar rs1/rs2 low selects when count_q is nonzero and raw-response low selects otherwise, using precisely the instruction's existing head/fallthrough selector. Carry only register-address predecode, never sampled operand values: actual RF state and WB write-first bypass must be observed on decode admission after arbitrary holds. Preserve the original selected five-bit source tags for forwarding, hazards, x0, bank selection and RVFI. The low-select sidecar is functional metadata associated with those exact tags, including unused instruction source-field aliases.\n\nIn RF, organize fixed-index words 0..15 and 16..31 into two read banks. Mask each word only with the incoming low-select bit indexed by register_number modulo sixteen. Read both banks in parallel with balanced OR reductions, then select bank zero or one using the original selected source address bit four. Apply the existing x0 priority and same-cycle WB bypass independently on both ports, without changing their architectural semantics. Keep reset-over-write priority and existing write decoders. There is no bank conflict: any pair of sources, equal or different, within or across banks is legal in one cycle.\n\nUse explicit fixed-index pair leaves and balanced reductions. A pair of (word_bit & low_select) terms fits a LUT4; do not reintroduce the high bank bit or full five-bit equality into these leaves. The low selection must arrive directly from IF sidecar/fallthrough predecode, never be re-decoded from the muxed binary source address. Keep high-bank selection after the local read tree. Retain explicit per-word flip-flops, not arrays eligible for RAM inference or synchronous read-output absorption. Add no RF output register or pipeline stage.\n\nWire the two low-select vectors from IF directly into RF in core. ID still receives the original selected instruction/PC/prediction and current RF operands, and captures exactly as before. Preserve queue acquisition and flush rules, accepted ready/unready JALR landing behavior, direct recovery, load/multiply replay, retained WB bypass during stalls, eight-edge division, real M arithmetic and ALTOPS, decoder default-illegal rules, all traps, contiguous retirement and exact-once memory effects. Keep nret=1, core's complete top-level IO, memory bounds, every original assertion and ready-high JALR/branch-to-divide retirement deadline. Change no evaluator, benchmark, formal assumption/depth, wrapper or core.yaml contract.\n\nValidate each selected low vector against one shifted by the corresponding admitted instruction's source low bits in the independent deque model, including every occupancy, empty bypass, full hold/pop/refill, redirects, changing unready bus data, reset and indirect landings. Keep all RF assertions, adapting only fixture wiring to derive low selects independently from binary test addresses. Exercise all 1,024 address pairs, same/cross-bank sources, x0, all destinations and WB bypass on either/both ports against the independent RF model. Run original pipeline tests, unchanged single-retire formal and full selftest/random RVFI comparisons in ready-high and stalled modes. Require canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds validation and the same 4,052,037 marker-bracketed cycles.\n\nMeasure all three vendor Gowin placements. Verify that occupancy no longer traverses binary low-source equality before the RF read reduction, that bank selection remains late, and that the RF still uses zero BSRAMs with unchanged writable-word storage. The nominal overhead is 128 sidecar FFs, partly pruned by synthesis, with less read logic than duplicating full RF networks. Qualification requires fitness above 226.42762 iter/sec or approximately 91.74931 MHz at unchanged cycles. Shared low-select fanout, sidecar placement, selected-address WB bypass and competing EX/divider paths are the main risks. This expected gain requires the complete topology and full evaluator gates; the earlier read-network probes do not establish a qualifying improvement.'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':7,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes']=[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Predecode the raw response source low four bits into two sixteen-bit one-hot vectors, carry their 32-bit sidecar through the existing four payload banks, and select head versus fallthrough metadata exactly with the admitted instruction. Keep speculative payload enables and occupancy-only flush semantics.'},\n {'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'Accept the two source low-select vectors and form two independent sixteen-word local read banks per source, selecting the final bank with the unchanged selected binary source high bit. Preserve explicit shared FF storage, fixed-index balanced reductions, x0, write-first bypass, reset and the write port.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire IF low-source predecode directly into RF while preserving the selected binary source tags, ID/EX admission, forwarding, hazard control and every top-level port.'},\n {'file':'test/test_if_stage_queue.py','description':'Keep the existing independent deque assertions and check selected low predecode against the selected instruction across all occupancies, pop/refill, full holds, redirects, reset, bus stalls and ready/unready landing responses.'},\n {'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'Adapt fixture wiring to independently derive predecode from binary test sources, preserving every assertion and all address-pair, same/cross-bank, write isolation, x0, bypass and synchronous reset tests.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain all architectural and latency checks and add source-sidecar association cases across queue/fallthrough transitions, dependency replay, WB aliases, prolonged holds, reset and wrong-path flushes.'}]\nclass D(yaml.SafeDumper):pass\ndef rep(dumper,s):return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',s,style='|' if '\\\\n' in s or len(s)>160 else None)\nD.add_representer(str,rep)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=D,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Schema-valid final architecture written:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_65", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r12s1.yaml');d=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Two-bank operand delivery with queued source predecode'\nd['motivation']='''The champion scores 216.47 iter/sec at 87.715 MHz with 4,052,037 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path runs from fetch-queue occupancy through the RF into ID/EX rs2: 16.717 ns and ten cells. Queue selection currently precedes binary source decode and the complete read tree. The successful r3s1 structural attempt established explicit RF flip-flops and validity-only squash; preserve both. Isolated four-candidate RF reads preserved CoreMark cycles but used 7,256 logic cells and reached only 83.089 MHz median. Moving the high-select gate after four eight-word reductions used 5,806 cells and reached 85.950 MHz. The proposed topology instead combines a two-bank read organization with queued low-address predecode to remove occupancy-dependent decoding without four full read networks.'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Restructure the fetch-to-RF operand boundary as two sixteen-word RF read banks fed by source predecode carried with queued instructions. This is one structural operand-delivery architecture. It keeps one physical 31-word flip-flop RF, two combinational read ports, one write port, the existing ID/EX capture edge and every instruction's latency. The two parts are complementary: predecode removes the binary-address comparator after queue selection; late bank selection keeps that predecode to sixteen bits per source rather than thirty-two and shares a single pair of physical read networks.\n\nIn IF compute sixteen one-hot low-offset selects for each raw source field: rs1 uses imem_data[18:15] and rs2 uses imem_data[23:20]. Predecode the raw response independently of count, stall, redirect, reset and validity. Carry the resulting 32-bit sidecar through four fixed fetch-queue banks, shifting and appending under exactly the same payload movement/enqueue conditions as instruction, PC and prediction. Append must win over shifting at its destination, including sole-entry replacement and full pop/refill. Like the existing speculative payload, sidecar storage has no reset/recovery gates: occupancy alone determines reachability. Do not alter the existing packed if_id_t layout or its validity semantics.\n\nPresent head-sidecar rs1/rs2 low selects when count_q is nonzero and raw-response low selects otherwise, using precisely the instruction's existing head/fallthrough selector. Carry only register-address predecode, never sampled operand values: actual RF state and WB write-first bypass must be observed on decode admission after arbitrary holds. Preserve the original selected five-bit source tags for forwarding, hazards, x0, bank selection and RVFI. The low-select sidecar is functional metadata associated with those exact tags, including unused instruction source-field aliases.\n\nIn RF, organize fixed-index words 0..15 and 16..31 into two read banks. Mask each word only with the incoming low-select bit indexed by register_number modulo sixteen. Read both banks in parallel with balanced OR reductions, then select bank zero or one using the original selected source address bit four. Apply the existing x0 priority and same-cycle WB bypass independently on both ports, without changing their architectural semantics. Keep reset-over-write priority and existing write decoders. There is no bank conflict: any pair of sources, equal or different, within or across banks is legal in one cycle.\n\nUse explicit fixed-index pair leaves and balanced reductions. A pair of (word_bit & low_select) terms fits a LUT4; do not reintroduce the high bank bit or full five-bit equality into these leaves. The low selection must arrive directly from IF sidecar/fallthrough predecode, never be re-decoded from the muxed binary source address. Keep high-bank selection after the local read tree. Retain explicit per-word flip-flops, not arrays eligible for RAM inference or synchronous read-output absorption. Add no RF output register or pipeline stage.\n\nWire the two low-select vectors from IF directly into RF in core. ID still receives the original selected instruction/PC/prediction and current RF operands, and captures exactly as before. Preserve queue acquisition and flush rules, accepted ready/unready JALR landing behavior, direct recovery, load/multiply replay, retained WB bypass during stalls, eight-edge division, real M arithmetic and ALTOPS, decoder default-illegal rules, all traps, contiguous retirement and exact-once memory effects. Keep nret=1, core's complete top-level IO, memory bounds, every original assertion and ready-high JALR/branch-to-divide retirement deadline. Change no evaluator, benchmark, formal assumption/depth, wrapper or core.yaml contract.\n\nValidate each selected low vector against one shifted by the corresponding admitted instruction's source low bits in the independent deque model, including every occupancy, empty bypass, full hold/pop/refill, redirects, changing unready bus data, reset and indirect landings. Keep all RF assertions, adapting only fixture wiring to derive low selects independently from binary test addresses. Exercise all 1,024 address pairs, same/cross-bank sources, x0, all destinations and WB bypass on either/both ports against the independent RF model. Run original pipeline tests, unchanged single-retire formal and full selftest/random RVFI comparisons in ready-high and stalled modes. Require canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds validation and the same 4,052,037 marker-bracketed cycles.\n\nMeasure all three vendor Gowin placements. Verify that occupancy no longer traverses binary low-source equality before the RF read reduction, that bank selection remains late, and that the RF still uses zero BSRAMs with unchanged writable-word storage. The nominal overhead is 128 sidecar FFs, partly pruned by synthesis, with less read logic than duplicating full RF networks. Qualification requires fitness above 226.42762 iter/sec or approximately 91.74931 MHz at unchanged cycles. Shared low-select fanout, sidecar placement, selected-address WB bypass and competing EX/divider paths are the main risks. This expected gain requires the complete topology and full evaluator gates; the earlier read-network probes do not establish a qualifying improvement.'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':7,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes']=[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Predecode the raw response source low four bits into two sixteen-bit one-hot vectors, carry their 32-bit sidecar through the existing four payload banks, and select head versus fallthrough metadata exactly with the admitted instruction. Keep speculative payload enables and occupancy-only flush semantics.'},\n {'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'Accept the two source low-select vectors and form two independent sixteen-word local read banks per source, selecting the final bank with the unchanged selected binary source high bit. Preserve explicit shared FF storage, fixed-index balanced reductions, x0, write-first bypass, reset and the write port.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire IF low-source predecode directly into RF while preserving the selected binary source tags, ID/EX admission, forwarding, hazard control and every top-level port.'},\n {'file':'test/test_if_stage_queue.py','description':'Keep the existing independent deque assertions and check selected low predecode against the selected instruction across all occupancies, pop/refill, full holds, redirects, reset, bus stalls and ready/unready landing responses.'},\n {'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'Adapt fixture wiring to independently derive predecode from binary test sources, preserving every assertion and all address-pair, same/cross-bank, write isolation, x0, bypass and synchronous reset tests.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain all architectural and latency checks and add source-sidecar association cases across queue/fallthrough transitions, dependency replay, WB aliases, prolonged holds, reset and wrong-path flushes.'}]\nclass D(yaml.SafeDumper):pass\ndef rep(dumper,s):return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',s,style='|' if '\\\\n' in s or len(s)>160 else None)\nD.add_representer(str,rep)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=D,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Schema-valid final architecture written:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Schema-valid final architecture written: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r12s1.yaml\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
