{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_61","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -u - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/555e5898/69cc8ea0/rf_ram_probe')\nr=build_all(Path.cwd(),p/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',p/'timing')\n(p/'timing_results.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2))\nprint(json.dumps(summarize(r),indent=2),flush=True)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"placement_failed\": false,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 82.839,\n  \"seeds\": [\n    80.347,\n    82.839,\n    82.839\n  ],\n  \"perturbations\": [\n    [\n      0,\n      80.347,\n      14\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      82.839,\n      15\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      82.839,\n      15\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"lut4\": 4669,\n  \"ff\": 1139,\n  \"lutram\": 32,\n  \"bsram\": 4,\n  \"dsp\": 6,\n  \"logic_levels\": 14,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_12_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_if/count_q_0_s2\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -7.446,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 17.74,\n    \"logic_cells\": 30,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_fwd\",\n      \"cpu/u_if\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_64", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r13s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-002-r13s1',\n'title':'Asynchronous LUT-RAM register file with reset-valid tracking',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 216.47 iter/sec at 87.715 MHz and 4,052,037 marked\nCoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path crosses fetch occupancy and RF\nreads into ID/EX operand capture. Last-round duplicate-read networks and\nsource-predecode sidecars lost timing through added LUTs and queue-control\nfanout. Replace the RF storage organization itself to shrink read/routing\ncost, without changing the fetch queue or pipeline. The earlier r3s1 fix\nremoved synchronous BSRAM output absorption; this proposal instead uses\nasynchronous distributed LUT-RAM with separate reset-valid tracking.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace the 31 writable 32-bit FF words and their full masked-OR read\nnetworks with replicated asynchronous distributed RAM. Make two identical\nphysical RF copies, one for each read port, written identically by the\nexisting single WB port. Split each copy into two 16-word by 32-bit banks;\nread both banks using the source address low four bits and select the\nfinal bank using address bit four. Every pair of source registers remains\navailable in the same cycle, including two addresses in the same bank.\n\nUse generic synthesizable SV arrays with synchronous writes and genuinely\ncombinational reads, plus Gowin's distributed-RAM inference attribute.\nDo not reset or initialize the RAM arrays and do not add RAM read/output\nregisters. Use a 32-bit per-word written-valid FF vector with x0 permanently\ninvalid. Synchronous reset clears the valid vector; an actual nonzero WB\nwrite sets its destination bit. A read of an unwritten register returns\nzero regardless of arbitrary physical RAM contents. RAM data writes may\noccur during reset harmlessly, since reset has priority in valid tracking\nand makes those contents unobservable until a subsequent actual write.\nBoth RAM copies always receive identical address/data/enables. Do not use\nan epoch bit whose wrap could expose stale data after repeated resets.\n\nPreserve the exact read priority independently on each port: x0 returns\nzero first, a same-cycle enabled matching WB write returns w_data second,\notherwise return RAM data if the selected register is valid and zero if\ninvalid. The WB bypass must work even for a destination's first post-reset\nwrite, before its valid bit rises. Ignore writes to x0 in both RAM copies\nand valid tracking. Keep reset synchronous and preserve all existing RF\nport semantics, raw unused-source aliases, and reset-over-write behavior.\nMask unknown/uninitialized RAM contents with validity so no X reaches\nobservable operands before their first write.\n\nKeep source addresses from the existing selected instruction and preserve\nall queue capacity, bank movement, fallthrough, branch prediction/recovery,\nready/unready JALR landing, hazard/replay, forwarding and instruction\nlatencies. Keep ID/EX as the real operand capture boundary outside RAM.\nThe storage must infer asynchronous RAM16/SSRAM resources, never BSRAM\nwith a synchronous output or absorption of ID/EX registers. Confirm this\nin Gowin synthesis/resource reports rather than assuming an attribute\nworked. Reuse the original selected address tags for hazards and RVFI.\nNo source sidecar, added general pipeline stage or duplicate FF RF is\npart of the proposal.\n\nPreserve nret=1, every top-level IO, contiguous exact-once retirement,\nexact-once memory effects, retained WB forwarding under dmem stalls,\neight-edge division, MEM multiplication, real M arithmetic and ALTOPS,\ndefault-illegal decode, all trap/alignment rules and memory bounds.\nChange no wrapper, formal assumption/depth, evaluator, benchmark or\ncore.yaml contract, and retain every existing test assertion.\n\nRun existing exhaustive RF tests and add independently modeled sequences\nwith repeated resets after filling every register, all 1,024 source pairs,\nunwritten-versus-written reads, same/cross-bank sources, writes to x0,\nfirst post-reset WB bypass on either/both ports, reset coincident with WB,\nand random reads/writes/reset events. Check both copies indirectly by\nreading each written value on both ports. Run all pipeline tests including\nunused raw-field RVFI aliases, prolonged memory/M-engine holds, replay,\nbranch/JALR and the existing ten-clock branch-to-divide bound. Run unchanged\nsingle-retire formal and complete ready-high/stalled selftest/random RVFI\ncomparisons plus canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds validation.\n\nThe intended stalled CoreMark cycle count is unchanged at 4,052,037.\nMeasure all three Gowin placements and require fitness above 226.42762\niter/sec, approximately 91.75 MHz at unchanged cycles. Nominal writable\nRF FF storage falls from 992 bits to 31 validity bits, at the cost of two\ncoherent RAM copies (about 2 Kbit total) and bank/bypass/valid selection.\nUse only resources inside the existing FPGA budget. The main risks are\nGowin mapping the arrays to FFs or BSRAM, distributed-memory routing, and\nvalid/bypass selection replacing the old RF timing bottleneck. The expected\ngain is an estimate until actual vendor timing and all correctness gates\nconfirm it.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Replace explicit FF words and masked-OR read reductions with two\nidentically written asynchronous distributed-RAM copies, each split into\ntwo 16x32 banks. Add reset-cleared per-word validity, preserve x0 and\nsame-cycle WB priority, and retain the complete existing RF interface.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Preserve every RF assertion and exhaustive source/write coverage. Add\nrepeated-reset tests over previously filled RAM, unwritten reads, first\npost-reset write-first bypass, reset/write coincidence and randomized\nreference-model checks that exercise both replicated read ports.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all pipeline/RVFI/M-engine/latency checks. Add repeated reset with\npreviously live RF values, both-port WB aliases, first-write dependencies,\nlong bus/M-engine holds and branch/JALR uses of replicated RAM values.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Complete LUT-RAM structural hypothesis written and schema valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
