{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_66", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id']=='hyp-20261002-002-r11s1':\n  s=d['hypothesis']['hypothesis']\n  print(s)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Restructure fetch state around parallel next, held and recovery address\nbanks and speculative occupancy, with a small registered selection token.\nCapture candidate recovery\naddresses on the very edge that currently updates the PC on redirect;\nselect the captured bank immediately after that edge. This separates\nforwarded target arithmetic from branch comparison, alignment checking\nand redirect qualification without adding a recovery cycle. It is one\nstructural change to fetch/decode recovery-state storage, retaining\nthe predictor, four-entry fetch queue and all execution latencies.\n\nIn EX expose two unqualified address candidates: resolved_target is\nJALR's wrapping rs1+imm with bit zero cleared for JALR, otherwise pc+imm;\nfallthrough_target is wrapping pc+4. The resolved-target data selection\ndepends only on the decoded JALR bit, never branch_cond, direct_taken,\nmisalign_fault, divider completion, reset or downstream acceptance.\nExpose the existing predicted_taken&&!direct_taken decision as a\ncorrection-kind bit, used only when the existing qualified redirect\nevent is true. Preserve the exact existing branch/jump alignment traps,\nreg_write suppression and next-PC/RVFI behavior. Keep the existing\nselected redirect_target available for verification if useful, but\ndisconnect it from functional fetch-address storage.\n\nIn IF add resolved_target_q and fallthrough_target_q, both sampled on\nevery clock edge with no reset, hold, redirect or request-valid gates.\nThey cannot be selected until a valid accepted correction captures a\ncorresponding token on that same edge. Use a registered token with\nNEXT, HELD, RESOLVED and FALLTHROUGH states, preferably mutually exclusive\none-hot flags. Reset selects HELD. An accepted redirect selects the\nappropriate recovery bank; otherwise storage_accept selects NEXT and\nno acquisition selects HELD. Every non-reset edge sets the whole token\nafresh, with redirect taking priority over acquisition.\n\nThe current architectural fetch address A is the token-selected bank,\nand drives io_imemAddr, pr\n…[6431 of 9503 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:eb8b672446914fe9]…\ny 4,052,037 cycles. Those\npartial topologies are not the delivered hypothesis: both retain a late\nstorage_accept/hold mux before the normal PC register. The complete\nfour-bank prototype measured 83.476 MHz and passed all 53 formal checks\nand pipeline/queue tests, but the residual path ended at ID/EX validity.\nThe delivered hypothesis completes isolation with the separate decode\nsquash sideband and branch-independent forwarding eligibility described\nabove. Before independent dequeue control, the decode-token prototype\npassed pipeline/queue tests, eight full-RVFI cosim runs and canonical\nCoreMark in 4,052,037 cycles, but regressed to 81.692 MHz median. Its\nworst path was stored_count_q through IF/hazard back to a payload-bank\nwrite, 17.509 ns through 12 cells. The delivered hypothesis additionally\nremoves that redundant readiness feedback via the independent dequeue\nhandshake. Complete-proposal timing has not been measured; earlier probes\ndo not establish a gain above the required 4.6 percent acceptance gate.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
