.37% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 (improvement, +522.03%): A shared eight-edge divider with four restoring steps per clock breaks the combinational / and % chain feeding the EX result mux, making the Fmax gain outweigh blocking DIV/REM latency in CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r2s1 (improvement, +30.03%): Separating a four-bit launch prefix with high-divisor guards from two radix\n…[7099 of 10171 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:67d8b73dc43ddea9]…\nedian Fmax from 87.715 to 79.265 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r12s1 (regression, -10.32%): Queued one-hot source predecode and late two-bank RF selection remove RF reads from reported worst paths, but 128 sidecar FFs add capture-enable fanout, making count_q through source-hazard/hold/append logic into sources3_q critical and reducing median Fmax to 78.660 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r13s1 (regression, +2.49%): Reset-free replicated asynchronous RF banks with written-valid tracking infer 32 Gowin RAM16s and reduce RF storage to 31 FFs, but forwarded EX/MEM results feeding fetch-PC recovery become critical, limiting throughput improvement to 2.49% at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r13s2 (regression, -6.25%): Even a 48-bit, eight-bank predictor with cached agreement lookup can worsen operand/forwarding-to-fetch count_q timing, making a median Fmax drop from 87.715 to 81.152 MHz outweigh 1.32% fewer CoreMark cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"|r11s2|r12s1|r13s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261001-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move division out of the combinational EX path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline's measured worst path is 199.837 ns through forwarding and\\nu_ex/u_alu, with 156 cells, and median Fmax is only 5.442 MHz. The ALU\\ncurrently implements signed and unsigned division and remainder with\\ncombinational / and % operators. These feed the shared EX result mux,\\nso their delay constrains every instruction, even when CoreMark executes\\nordinary integer operations. There are no recorded structural attempts\\nor LESSONS.md entries for this core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline's measured worst path is 199.837 ns through forwarding and\\nu_ex/u_alu, with 156 cells, and median Fmax is only 5.442 MHz. The ALU\\ncurrently implements signed and unsigned division and remainder with\\ncombinational / and % operators. These feed the shared EX result mux,\\nso their delay constrains every instruction, even when CoreMark executes\\nordinary integer operations. There are no recorded structural attempts\\nor LESSONS.md entries for this core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU hardware with one shared\\nblocking divider integrated into EX. Use a registered unsigned restoring\\nquotient/remainder datapath with four fixed restoring steps per clock:\\neight iteration clocks replace the 32-step combinational division chain.\\nNormalize signed operands and execute the first four-bit chunk on the\\nlaunch edge, then execute the remaining seven chunks on subsequent edges.\\nLatch the forwarded operands and operation, and correct quotient/remainder\\nsigns from registered results without adding a correction state.\\nPreserve division by zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics exactly. MUL variants\\nand ordinary ALU operations retain their existing single-cycle behavior.\\n\\nAdd explicit launch, busy, result-valid and consume handshakes. Launch\\nonly for a valid divide/remainder in EX when downstream backpressure\\npermits it. Hold PC and ID/EX until the result \n…[188683 of 191755 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a9851027d2e0b4b8]…\nt\": 2.49, \"vs_baseline\": 1730.45, \"fmax_mhz\": 89.896, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4052037, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 4643, \"ff\": 1139, \"seeds\": [84.753, 89.896, 89.896], \"perturbations\": [[0, 84.753, 13], [1, 89.896, 12], [2, 89.896, 12]], \"lutram\": 32, \"bsram\": 4, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_6_s0\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_if/resident_pc_q_2_s0\", \"slack_ns\": -6.799, \"arrival_ns\": 17.093, \"logic_cells\": 35, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_fwd\", \"cpu/u_if\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 13, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T00:35:25.541565Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r13s1 (regression, +2.49%): Reset-free replicated asynchronous RF banks with written-valid tracking infer 32 Gowin RAM16s and reduce RF storage to 31 FFs, but forwarded EX/MEM results feeding fetch-PC recovery become critical, limiting throughput improvement to 2.49% at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
