{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r['category']=='predictor':\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],'fitness',r.get('fitness'),'cycles',r.get('cycles'),'Fmax',r.get('fmax_mhz'))\n  if r['id'] in ['hyp-20261001-003-r2s2','hyp-20261001-003-r9s2','hyp-20261001-003-r10s2','hyp-20261002-003-r13s2']:\n   h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n   print(h.get('motivation','') if isinstance(h,dict) else '')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 Small tagged next-PC predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis fitness 13.26 cycles 4208674 Fmax 5.579\nhyp-20261001-003-r2s2 Direction-only branch prediction with decoded direct targets fitness 87.61 cycles 4201740 Fmax 36.81\nThe champion already removes combinational division from the critical\npath and reaches 33.863 MHz, but fetch still assumes PC+4 and loses one\nyounger instruction slot on every taken branch or jump. The IF/ID bundle\nis combinational, so the real correction penalty is one slot, not the\ntwo slots suggested by the five-stage comments. The earlier predictor\nattempt reduced CoreMark cycles from 4,491,485 to 4,208,674, demonstrating\nuseful branch locality, but its approximately 5.6 MHz divider-dominated\nimplementation did not retain the champion's eight-edge divider.\nUse the current champion RTL as the base. A direction-only table can\nexploit that locality without storing or selecting wide tagged targets.\n\nA fresh reference-ISS profile of the canonical ten-iteration, 2K, -O3\nCoreMark run counted 2,922,016 instructions inside the MMIO markers,\nincluding 529,588 conditional branches, 49,367 JALs, and 13,735 JALRs.\nSequential prediction misses 357,527 next PCs. A 64-entry direction\ntable plus decoded JAL targets missed 65,592, including unpredicted\nJALRs, saving an estimated 291,935 correction slots. Against the\nchampion's 4,491,485 cycles, this estimates 6.95% higher throughput\nat constant Fmax. This model updates counters immediately, omits\npipeline/stall replay, and is not an RTL or FPGA measurement. The\nreference execution matched every canonical CRC and validation text.\n\nhyp-20261001-003-r3s2 Bimodal direct prediction with one-bit EX recovery fitness 103.09 cycles 4201740 Fmax 43.316\nhyp-20261001-003-r4s2 Static backward-taken prediction with one-bit recovery fitness 121.32 cycles 4259699 Fmax 51.68\nhyp-20261001-003-r5s2 Static-biased exception predictor with deferred training fitness 117.01 cycles 4227597 Fmax 49.467\nhyp-20261001-003-r6s2 Prediction-guided fetch lookahead du\n…[3248 of 6320 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7ddb5f2f79c256fd]…\nRegistered prediction recovery with an indirect landing bypass fitness 216.47 cycles 4052037 Fmax 87.715\nhyp-20261002-003-r12s2 Parallel operand reads for the prediction-guided fetch queue fitness 200.56 cycles 4052037 Fmax 81.267\nhyp-20261002-003-r13s2 One-bit local correlation with a cached agreement lookup fitness 202.95 cycles 3998554 Fmax 81.152\nThe champion reaches 216.47 iterations/second at 87.715 MHz and\n4,052,037 marked CoreMark cycles with stateless BTFNT/JAL prediction.\nEarlier agreement and local-history predictors saved about two percent\nof cycles, but the 64-bank local-history implementation added 704\nstate bits and exposed fetch-PC/predictor feedback as a timing limit.\nRecent parallel operand reads also lost timing. Capture short repeating\nbranch patterns with a much smaller predictor and a single cached-bit\nlookup, keeping the champion's entire queue and recovery topology.\n\nhyp-20261002-003-r14s2 Registered correction with same-cycle target admission fitness 214.92 cycles 4052037 Fmax 87.087\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"The learned direction predictors saved only 1–2% of CoreMark cycles and often reduced Fmax. I’m examining the prediction-recovery path for a timing gain without adding another fetch table."}}
